J.A.R.V.I.S. deployment — Phase 4 (Zero-knowledge proofs) evidence
Date: 2026-10-21
Build: REFERENCE implementation — NOT PRODUCTION CRYPTOGRAPHY
  (see phase4/proofs.py header for the honesty notice)

Statements (exactly three, standardized):
  S1: This value exceeds threshold T in sealed baseline B
      (variance-gate alerts without exposing the baseline)
  S2: An approval exists for intent digest D
      (actuation without re-showing the approval)
  S3: This payload satisfies policy P
      (execution without exposing the payload)

Protocol shape: commit -> challenge -> respond -> verify,
Fiat-Shamir (challenge = SHA-256 of transcript), non-interactive.
Freshness window: 300s; proofs bind ts + proof_nonce.

TESTS: Ran 35 tests — PASS (OK)
  Breakdown: round-trip S1/S2/S3 (4 incl. wire-privacy),
  false-statement refusal (7 incl. seal-key + tampered approval),
  tampered proof (5), superseded circuit (4), freshness (4),
  replay (2), registry (7), budget measurements (1),
  reference-tag presence folded into round-trip.

MEASURED vs §4.4 BUDGETS (200 iterations per statement):
  stmt | proving ms (budget 30000) | verify ms (budget 100) | proof bytes (budget 204800 STARK / 2048 SNARK)
  S1   |      0.027                |      0.019             | 687
  S2   |      0.041                |      0.020             | 656
  S3   |      0.038                |      0.019             | 675

  All budgets met with large margins. Proof sizes (~0.66 KB)
  also fall under the 2 KB SNARK budget numerically, but the
  reference is NOT a SNARK/STARK — no such claim is made.

HONEST LIMITS:
  1. Zero-knowledge property NOT established for the reference:
     the blinded opening is reversible by the verifier.
  2. The statement predicate is evaluated by the (honest) prover
     and attested in the transcript; a dishonest prover could
     attest falsely. The wire shape cannot detect that — hence
     the prover refuses false statements (ProveError, fail-closed).
  3. Production requires audited STARK/SNARK circuits per §4.3
     (STARKs preferred: no trusted setup).
  4. A proof proves the statement, not the data's truth (§4.5).
     Proofs compose with seals (S2 verifies the approval seal
     before attesting); they do not replace them.

